  "activity": {
    "id": "vsepr_theory_lab",
    "name": "VSEPR Theory: Predicting Molecular Geometry from Lewis Structures",
    "description": "A comprehensive AR laboratory experience guiding 100-level chemistry students through VSEPR theory. Students explore how electron pairs around a central atom minimize repulsion, discover the five fundamental electron-domain geometries, learn to distinguish electron-domain geometry from molecular geometry, understand why lone pairs occupy more angular space, and apply VSEPR predictions to real molecules including CO2, BF3, CH4, PF5, SF6, NH3, H2O, and XeF4.",
    "language": "en",
    "workplace": "vsepr_lab_workplace",
    "start": "step_1_intro",
    "actions": [
      {
        "id": "step_1_intro",
        "viewport": "actions",
        "type": "actions",
        "device": "ar_headset",
        "location": "main_lab_workspace",
        "predicate": "vsepr_intro_narration",
        "instruction": {
          "title": "Welcome to VSEPR Theory Lab",
          "description": "VSEPR theory predicts the three-dimensional shape of covalent molecules. The core idea is simple: electron pairs around a central atom repel one another, and molecules adopt whatever geometry places these electron domains as far apart as possible. In this lab, you will start from a Lewis structure, count electron domains (bonding pairs and lone pairs), and predict the actual 3D molecular shape. You will explore five fundamental electron-domain geometries: linear, trigonal planar, tetrahedral, trigonal bipyramidal, and octahedral. Let's begin by understanding how to count electron domains from a Lewis structure."
        },
        "enter": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [
            {
              "target": "lewis_structure_panel",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "water_lewis_structure",
              "poi": "water_lewis_poi"
            },
            {
              "target": "learner_student",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "vsepr_intro_narration"
            }
          ],
          "deactivate": [],
          "messages": [],
          "if_logic": []
        },
        "triggers": [
          {
            "mode": "click",
            "id": "continue_step_1",
            "type": "action",
            "viewport": "actions",
            "operator": "equal"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "step_2_minimum_repulsion",
        "viewport": "actions",
        "type": "actions",
        "device": "ar_headset",
        "location": "main_lab_workspace",
        "predicate": "minimum_repulsion_animation",
        "instruction": {
          "title": "The Minimum-Repulsion Principle in Action",
          "description": "Watch as electron domains arrange themselves to minimize repulsion. We start with two domains that drift apart until they are 180 degrees apart\u2014a linear arrangement. Then a third domain is added; the three rearrange into a trigonal planar geometry at 120-degree intervals. A fourth domain produces a tetrahedral arrangement with angles of approximately 109.5 degrees. Each time a new domain is added, you will see faint repulsion vectors between domains as they settle into their lowest-energy configuration. This animation demonstrates the fundamental driving force of VSEPR theory."
        },
        "enter": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [
            {
              "target": "central_atom_sphere",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "electron_domain_cloud",
              "poi": "label_poi"
            },
            {
              "target": "central_atom_sphere",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "minimum_repulsion_animation",
              "poi": "default"
            },
            {
              "target": "learner_student",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "electron_geometry_explanation"
            }
          ],
          "deactivate": [
            {
              "target": "lewis_structure_panel",
              "type": "*"
            }
          ],
          "messages": [],
          "if_logic": []
        },
        "triggers": [
          {
            "mode": "voice",
            "id": "continue_step_2",
            "type": "action",
            "viewport": "actions",
            "operator": "equal"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "step_3_five_geometries",
        "viewport": "actions",
        "type": "actions",
        "device": "ar_headset",
        "location": "main_lab_workspace",
        "predicate": "geometry_summary_panel",
        "instruction": {
          "title": "The Five Electron-Domain Geometries",
          "description": "There are five fundamental electron-domain geometries, determined solely by the number of electron domains around the central atom. With 2 domains: linear (180\u00b0). With 3 domains: trigonal planar (120\u00b0). With 4 domains: tetrahedral (109.5\u00b0). With 5 domains: trigonal bipyramidal (axial 90\u00b0, equatorial 120\u00b0). With 6 domains: octahedral (all neighbors 90\u00b0). You can rotate the displayed geometries to view them from any angle. The underlying electron-domain geometry depends only on the steric number\u2014the total number of electron domains\u2014and does not change regardless of whether those domains are bonding pairs or lone pairs."
        },
        "enter": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [
            {
              "target": "main_lab_workspace",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "geometry_summary_panel",
              "poi": "default"
            },
            {
              "target": "central_atom_sphere",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "electron_domain_cloud",
              "poi": "default"
            }
          ],
          "deactivate": [],
          "messages": [],
          "if_logic": []
        },
        "triggers": [
          {
            "mode": "click",
            "id": "continue_step_3",
            "type": "action",
            "viewport": "actions",
            "operator": "equal"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "step_4_electron_vs_molecular",
        "viewport": "actions",
        "type": "actions",
        "device": "ar_headset",
        "location": "main_lab_workspace",
        "predicate": "methane_3d_model",
        "instruction": {
          "title": "Electron-Domain Geometry vs. Molecular Geometry",
          "description": "An important distinction: electron-domain geometry is the spatial arrangement of all electron pairs (bonding and lone pairs) around the central atom. Molecular geometry is the shape traced by the atoms alone\u2014lone pairs are invisible in the molecular shape. We start with methane (CH4): four bonding domains in a tetrahedral arrangement. Both electron-domain geometry and molecular geometry are tetrahedral. Now replace one hydrogen with a lone pair, creating ammonia (NH3). The electron-domain geometry remains tetrahedral, but the molecular geometry becomes trigonal pyramidal because we only count the three bonds in the shape. Finally, replace another hydrogen with a lone pair to form water (H2O): still a tetrahedral electron-domain geometry, but now the molecular geometry is bent. The underlying electron distribution has not changed; only what we see as the molecular shape has changed."
        },
        "enter": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [
            {
              "target": "central_atom_sphere",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "methane_3d_model",
              "poi": "default"
            },
            {
              "target": "central_atom_sphere",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "ammonia_3d_model",
              "poi": "default"
            },
            {
              "target": "central_atom_sphere",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "water_3d_model",
              "poi": "default"
            }
          ],
          "deactivate": [
            {
              "target": "main_lab_workspace",
              "type": "*"
            }
          ],
          "messages": [],
          "if_logic": []
        },
        "triggers": [
          {
            "mode": "voice",
            "id": "continue_step_4",
            "type": "action",
            "viewport": "actions",
            "operator": "equal"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "step_5_lone_pair_asymmetry",
        "viewport": "actions",
        "type": "actions",
        "device": "ar_headset",
        "location": "main_lab_workspace",
        "predicate": "lone_pair_explanation_narration",
        "instruction": {
          "title": "Why Lone Pairs Occupy More Angular Space",
          "description": "Lone pairs spread out more than bonding pairs because a lone pair is held only by the central nucleus, while a bonding pair is shared between two nuclei. This asymmetry means lone pairs occupy more angular space and compress the bond angles between the remaining bonding pairs. Methane (CH4) has four bonding pairs and H-C-H angles of 109.5\u00b0. Ammonia (NH3) has one lone pair and three bonding pairs, compressing the H-N-H angles to about 107\u00b0. Water (H2O) has two lone pairs and two bonding pairs, further compressing the H-O-H angle to 104.5\u00b0. The visualization shows all three molecules overlaid at the central atom so you can directly compare how the bond angle decreases as lone pairs are added. Toggle the overlay view to examine the progressive compression of bond angles."
        },
        "enter": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [
            {
              "target": "central_atom_sphere",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "bond_angle_comparison_visualization",
              "poi": "default"
            },
            {
              "target": "learner_student",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "lone_pair_explanation_narration"
            }
          ],
          "deactivate": [],
          "messages": [],
          "if_logic": []
        },
        "triggers": [
          {
            "mode": "click",
            "id": "continue_step_5",
            "type": "action",
            "viewport": "actions",
            "operator": "equal"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "step_6_molecule_gallery",
        "viewport": "actions",
        "type": "actions",
        "device": "ar_headset",
        "location": "molecule_display_area",
        "predicate": "gallery_narration",
        "instruction": {
          "title": "Gallery of Real Molecules Across All VSEPR Classes",
          "description": "You now see eight real molecules representing the full range of electron-domain geometries and the effects of lone pairs. Approach each molecule to view its Lewis structure and 3D structure side by side. CO2 (linear): 2 bonding domains, no lone pairs. BF3 (trigonal planar): 3 bonding domains, no lone pairs. CH4 (tetrahedral): 4 bonding domains, no lone pairs. PF5 (trigonal bipyramidal): 5 bonding domains, no lone pairs. SF6 (octahedral): 6 bonding domains, no lone pairs. NH3 (trigonal pyramidal): 4 domains total, 3 bonding and 1 lone pair. H2O (bent): 4 domains total, 2 bonding and 2 lone pairs. XeF4 (square planar): 6 domains total, 4 bonding and 2 lone pairs. For each, the narration will walk through the electron count, steric number, electron-domain geometry, lone pair effects, and resulting molecular geometry."
        },
